Task 1: Install Argo CD in Kubernetes
Subtask 1.1: Install Docker
First, we need to install Docker to support our containerized environment.

# Update the package index
sudo apt update

# Install required packages
sudo apt install -y apt-transport-https ca-certificates curl software-properties-common

# Add Docker's official GPG key
curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/docker-archive-keyring.gpg

# Add Docker repository
echo "deb [arch=amd64 signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/docker-archive-keyring.gpg] https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu $(lsb_release -cs) stable" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list > /dev/null

# Update package index again
sudo apt update

# Install Docker
sudo apt install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io

# Add current user to docker group
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

# Start and enable Docker service
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker

# Verify Docker installation
docker --version
Subtask 1.2: Install kubectl
Install the Kubernetes command-line tool to interact with our cluster.

# Download kubectl binary
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"

# Make kubectl executable
chmod +x kubectl

# Move kubectl to system PATH
sudo mv kubectl /usr/local/bin/

# Verify kubectl installation
kubectl version --client
Subtask 1.3: Install kind (Kubernetes in Docker)
We'll use kind to create a local Kubernetes cluster.

# Download kind binary
curl -Lo ./kind https://kind.sigs.k8s.io/dl/v0.20.0/kind-linux-amd64

# Make kind executable
chmod +x ./kind

# Move kind to system PATH
sudo mv ./kind /usr/local/bin/kind

# Verify kind installation
kind version
Subtask 1.4: Create Kubernetes Cluster
Create a local Kubernetes cluster using kind.

# Create a kind cluster
kind create cluster --name argocd-lab

# Verify cluster is running
kubectl cluster-info --context kind-argocd-lab

# Check nodes
kubectl get nodes
Subtask 1.5: Install Argo CD
Now we'll install Argo CD in our Kubernetes cluster.

# Create argocd namespace
kubectl create namespace argocd

# Install Argo CD
kubectl apply -n argocd -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml

# Wait for all pods to be ready (this may take a few minutes)
kubectl wait --for=condition=available --timeout=300s deployment/argocd-server -n argocd

# Verify all Argo CD components are running
kubectl get pods -n argocd
Subtask 1.6: Expose Argo CD Server
Make the Argo CD server accessible from outside the cluster.

# Patch the argocd-server service to use NodePort
kubectl patch svc argocd-server -n argocd -p '{"spec":{"type":"NodePort"}}'

# Get the NodePort assigned
kubectl get svc argocd-server -n argocd

# Port forward to access the UI (run in background)
kubectl port-forward svc/argocd-server -n argocd 8080:443 --address=0.0.0.0 &
Task 2: Explore Argo CD UI, API, and CLI
Subtask 2.1: Install Argo CD CLI
Install the Argo CD command-line interface.

# Download Argo CD CLI
curl -sSL -o argocd-linux-amd64 https://github.com/argoproj/argo-cd/releases/latest/download/argocd-linux-amd64

# Make it executable
chmod +x argocd-linux-amd64

# Move to system PATH
sudo mv argocd-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/argocd

# Verify installation
argocd version --client
Subtask 2.2: Access Argo CD UI
Get the initial admin password and access the web interface.

# Get the initial admin password
kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d && echo

# Note: Save this password for UI login
Important: The Argo CD UI is now accessible at https://localhost:8080. Use the following credentials:

Username: admin
Password: (the output from the command above)
Subtask 2.3: Login via CLI
Configure the CLI to connect to your Argo CD instance.

# Login to Argo CD via CLI (use the password from previous step)
argocd login localhost:8080 --username admin --password $(kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d) --insecure

# Verify login
argocd account get-user-info
Subtask 2.4: Explore CLI Commands
Familiarize yourself with basic Argo CD CLI commands.

# List available commands
argocd --help

# Get cluster information
argocd cluster list

# Get application information (should be empty initially)
argocd app list

# Get project information
argocd proj list

# Get repository information
argocd repo list
Subtask 2.5: Explore API Endpoints
Test basic API functionality using curl.

# Get API token for authentication
API_TOKEN=$(argocd account generate-token --account admin)

# Test API connectivity
curl -k -H "Authorization: Bearer $API_TOKEN" https://localhost:8080/api/v1/clusters

# Get application list via API
curl -k -H "Authorization: Bearer $API_TOKEN" https://localhost:8080/api/v1/applications

# Get project list via API
curl -k -H "Authorization: Bearer $API_TOKEN" https://localhost:8080/api/v1/projects
Task 3: Set up Initial Project in Argo CD
Subtask 3.1: Create a Sample Git Repository
We'll create a local Git repository with a sample application.

# Create a directory for our sample application
mkdir -p ~/argocd-lab/sample-app
cd ~/argocd-lab/sample-app

# Initialize Git repository
git init

# Create a simple Kubernetes deployment manifest
cat > deployment.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: sample-app
  labels:
    app: sample-app
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: sample-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: sample-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: sample-app
        image: nginx:1.21
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: sample-app-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: sample-app
  ports:
  - protocol: TCP
    port: 80
    targetPort: 80
  type: ClusterIP
EOF

# Create a simple ConfigMap
cat > configmap.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: sample-app-config
data:
  app.properties: |
    app.name=sample-application
    app.version=1.0.0
    environment=development
EOF

# Add files to Git
git add .
git config user.email "student@alnafi.com"
git config user.name "Lab Student"
git commit -m "Initial commit: Add sample application manifests"

# Get the repository path
REPO_PATH=$(pwd)
echo "Repository path: $REPO_PATH"
Subtask 3.2: Create Argo CD Project
Create a new project in Argo CD to organize our applications.

# Create project using CLI
argocd proj create lab-project \
    --description "Lab project for learning Argo CD" \
    --src "$REPO_PATH" \
    --dest https://kubernetes.default.svc,default \
    --allow-cluster-resource '*/*' \
    --allow-namespaced-resource '*/*'

# Verify project creation
argocd proj list

# Get detailed project information
argocd proj get lab-project
Subtask 3.3: Create Argo CD Application
Create an application that will sync our sample manifests.

# Create application using CLI
argocd app create sample-app \
    --repo "$REPO_PATH" \
    --path . \
    --dest-server https://kubernetes.default.svc \
    --dest-namespace default \
    --project lab-project \
    --sync-policy automated \
    --auto-prune \
    --self-heal

# Verify application creation
argocd app list

# Get detailed application information
argocd app get sample-app
Subtask 3.4: Sync and Deploy Application
Synchronize the application to deploy it to the cluster.

# Sync the application
argocd app sync sample-app

# Wait for sync to complete
argocd app wait sample-app --timeout 300

# Check application status
argocd app get sample-app

# Verify deployment in Kubernetes
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get services
kubectl get configmaps
kubectl get pods -l app=sample-app
Subtask 3.5: Test GitOps Workflow
Demonstrate the GitOps workflow by making changes to the repository.

# Navigate to repository directory
cd ~/argocd-lab/sample-app

# Modify the deployment to change replica count
sed -i 's/replicas: 2/replicas: 3/' deployment.yaml

# Commit the change
git add deployment.yaml
git commit -m "Scale up to 3 replicas"

# Refresh the application to detect changes
argocd app refresh sample-app

# Check if Argo CD detected the change
argocd app get sample-app

# Sync the changes
argocd app sync sample-app

# Verify the scaling occurred
kubectl get deployments sample-app
kubectl get pods -l app=sample-app
